<article class="env-example" id="02MW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02MW">Example <span data-tag="02MW">4.7.1.11</span> <span class="named">(Truncated Objects of Joins)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be $\infty $-categories, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\star \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ denote their join (Construction <a data-tag="016Y" href="/tag/016Y">4.3.3.13</a>). Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is $n$-truncated when viewed as an object of the join $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\star \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $n \geq -1$, then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated if and only if it is $n$-truncated when viewed as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\star \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> See Example <a data-tag="01J9" href="/tag/01J9">4.6.1.6</a>. </p>
</article>
